seg-de24fd02c7c2 = <c1/> is the one entry point the command line and every other surface call to make a pack: a scenario, the log's bytes (and the navigation file's, for RINEX), the window as text, a title and an optional creation time, plus the signing seed, in; the files, the epoch count, the slice, the manifest hash and the signer fingerprint out. It reads no files and no clock, so the same inputs give the same bytes. Below it, <c2/> and <c3/> are pure functions: bytes in (<c4/>, a 32-byte seed, a <c5/> of files), serialisable results out (<c6/>, <c7/>). They use no files, clock or process state, and build for <c8/>, so the Python, WebAssembly and server surfaces call them unchanged. The command line is a thin wrapper over them.

